    <paragraphs>
      <title>Responsibility for Learning</title>
      <p>
        Every student in this course is responsible for their own learning. 
        The instructor will make every effort to support you; however, if you need any help
        it is your responsibility to reach out either via email or by attending office hours. 
        You are responsible for checking your email daily for course announcements!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Course Content</title>
      <p>
        The primary goal of this course is to deepen your understanding of the geometry and measurement 
        (and, a bit of the probability and statistics) strand of K--8 mathematics. 
        This will often involve studying mathematics beyond the K--8 level to help future teachers gain a 
        better perspective of how the topics and concepts they teach fit into the broader mathematical structure. 
        Strengthening your understanding will help you to be more effective as a teacher. 
        The course will provide a careful, mathematically accurate development of geometry and measurement 
        (and a bit of probability and statistics) and help you teach the subject in a coherent and meaningful way. 
        Mathematics has a structure that is logical -- everything has a reason. 
        Understanding math well is the key to being able to teach it well. 
        Course topics include measurement, geometric figures, unknown angles, deductive geometry, area, 
        volume/surface area, similarity, introductory probability, and data analysis.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Required Course Materials</title>
      <p>
        There is one required text for this course:  
        \emph{Elementary Geometry for Teachers}, by Thomas H. Parker and Scott J. Baldridge. 
        The book is currently out of print, but a copy will be loaned to you for the semester; 
        you will need to return it. All other course documents, materials, and assignments will 
        be posted on MyOpenMath (MOM), a free online course management system specifically designed for mathematics. 
        You will need to create a MOM account and register for our course.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Calculator Use</title>
      <p>
        Calculators are not permitted to be used in this course.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>




  <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-132-grading">
    <title>Assignments and Grading</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Standards Based Grading</title>
      <p>
        In this course we will be using standards based grading, which is an alternative approach to grading that 
        emphasizes progress and growth rather than an arbitrary weighted average of all assessments. 
        Instead of using a percentage-based system, your grade will be determined by how well you display mastery 
        of specific sorts of mathematical tasks called Learning Targets (LTs). This type of grading system allows 
        students to make up for past poor performances by allowing for a limited number of reassessments without penalty, 
        provides real-time feedback on their current understanding of course objectives, and directs and emphasizes 
        long-term progress in key course objectives. All aspects of this course and its grading scheme will focus 
        around the 22 LTs presented at the end of this syllabus. In short, your grade will be determined by your 
        performance in three categories: preparation and participation, in-class quizzes, and a cumulative final exam; 
        detailed information about each of these categories, as well as how your course grade will be determined using 
        these categories, can be found in the following sections of this syllabus. You will likely find this system to 
        be unfamiliar, so I encourage you to ask questions about this approach to grading as soon as they arise! Please 
        see the ``Advantages of Standards Based Grading'' section of this syllabus for more information about why we are 
        using this system in this course!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Preparation and Participation</title>
      <p>
        This is the first category contributing towards your course grade. 
        There will be two primary types of assessments considered in this category:
      </p>

      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Reading Guides</title> 
          <p>
            A reading guide will be assigned to be completed before most class meetings. 
            Each will consist of a reading assignment from the required text and questions 
            designed to help you take good notes. Completing a reading guide before class 
            means you are prepared with all relevant definitions and basic examples worked 
            out in front of you, and allows us to spend class time discussing more difficult 
            aspects of the material and working together on problems! Our work in class will 
            assume you have completed the reading guide; failure to do so could contribute to 
            confusion about the course material. You will complete each reading guide in your 
            own notes, then scan your pages into a single PDF and submit through MOM. Reading 
            guides will be due by 11:59pm on Mondays and Wednesdays (i.e., the night before each 
            class meeting). No late attempts will be accepted for any reason and no reassessment 
            is allowed. Reading guides will be graded on completeness and effort as determined 
            by the instructor.
          </p> 
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Class Participation</title>
          <p>
            We will meet weekly on Tuesdays and Thursdays, 8:30--9:45am. Attendance and active participation, 
            including working collaboratively with classmates, during these meetings are expected. The class 
            meetings will consist of a mix of lecture and active learning activities based on the course 
            material from the reading guide. You will earn a weekly participation grade; to earn credit for 
            this, you must fully participate throughout the entire class meeting -- this means being in class 
            ready to go at 8:30am, having phones/laptops/other devices put away, and only your notes and other 
            material related to this course and the topic at hand on your desk.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Your overall Preparation and Participation score will be determined by your reading guide scores 
        and class participation scores averaged together.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Quizzes</title>

      <p>
        This category is based on your demonstration of this course's 22 learning targets as assessed on in-class quizzes given at the end of every Thursday class. 
        Each quiz question will address a single LT; you will receive a score for each individual question based on how well you showed your understanding of the 
        corresponding LT according to the following rubric:
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Score</cell>
          <cell>Description</cell>
        </row> 
        <row>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell>
            <alert>Mastery of the LT exceeding expections.</alert>
            Provides clear understanding with complete and clear communication. 
            Any error is trivial.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>
            <alert>Adequate understanding of the LT meeting expectations. </alert>
            Provides an adequate understanding with correct work and clear explanations. 
            Some revisions or expansion is needed, but no significant gaps or errors are present.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>
            <alert>Partial understanding of LT falling below expectations.</alert>
            Provides a partial understanding, but significant gaps remain.
            Needs further work, more review, and/or improved explanation.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>
            <alert>Little understanding of LT falling significantly below expectations.</alert>
            Provides little to no understanding with either fragmentary work, 
            significant errors or omissions, or too many errors.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>
            <alert>No understanding of the LT or LT was not attempted.</alert>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      
      <p>
        Each LT will be assessed twice on in-class quizzes before the end of the semester. 
        A limited number of reassessments will be allowed (see next paragraph for details). 
        Your grade for each LT will be the average of your highest two attempts among the two 
        in-class quizzes and any reassessment attempts you complete.
      </p>

      <p>
        In order to continually emphasize progress and growth, you may try to improve your score on any LT by reassessing on it. 
        In other words, if you did not perform well on a LT on an in-class quiz, you may reattempt a question on that LT again. 
        Reassessments can be taken in-class during a future quiz or in my office during office hours or other times set aside for reassessments. 
        \textbf{Reassessments must be scheduled at least 24 hours in advance using the Google Form posted on MOM} to ensure I will
         have the appropriate reassessment ready! You \textbf{must} submit this form 24 hours prior in order to be allowed to reassess; 
         simply showing up at my office will not suffice! You may reassess no more than two LTs per week. In addition to these weekly 
         reassessment opportunities, two of our Thursday class meetings, March 12 and April 30, will be dedicated ``reassessment days.'' 
         No new material will be covered on these days, instead the class time will be dedicated to allowing students to reassess on up to 
         10 LTs each in addition to the usual LT quiz for that class meeting. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's look at an example of how the LT Quizzes and reassessments will work!  
        Suppose Alex earns a 0 on a specific LT the first time it is assessed and a 3 the second time it is assessed; 
        at this point Alex's score for this LT is a 1.5 (the average of their two attempts, 0 and 3). 
        Alex then requests to reassess on this LT in an office hour the following week. 
        Alex drops by to reassess on the LT and earns a 4; now their score for this LT is a 3.5 
        (the average of the highest two attempts, 3 and 4) -- meaning the initial 0 is no longer counted! 
        In this example, Alex has learned from their mistake, and shown growth and progress towards mastery of this LT. 
        Their score reflects this growth!
      </p>

      <p>
        A collection of practice problems will be available for each of the LTs to provide a sample of the types of things 
        you might be asked on the LT Quizzes, as well as provide some zero-stakes practice of the various concepts we've discussed. 
        These practice problems will not be collected, nor will they contribute towards your course grade in any direct way; 
        however, in many cases, practice will be necessary to master the LTs in this course! I will happily provide feedback on 
        your work on any of the practice problems, if desired; simply email me your work. 
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Final Exam</title>
      
      <p>
        The third category is a cumulative final exam, which is scheduled for Thursday, May 7, 8:30 -- 11:30am, 
        the time indicated on the 
        <url href="https://www.worcester.edu/academics/catalogs-calendars/2025-26-academic-calendar/">WSU Spring 2026 Academic Calendar</url>. 
        The final exam will consist of 16 problems, each associated with one of the 22 LTs for this course. 
        Each problem will be scored using the same 0--4 rubric as the LT Quizzes. 
        There will be no reassessments for the final exam! A make-up final exam will be given at the instructor's discretion, 
        and only when there is a legitimate reason for missing the original final exam. Working or arriving late for 
        class are not legitimate reasons for missing the final!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Letter Grade Assignments</title>

      <p>
        Your final grade is based on completing a specific list of requirements. 
        Your final grade will be the highest grade whose criteria you <alert>fully</alert> satisfy.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In order to guarantee a grade of "A", you should attain all of the following:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Preparation and Participation:  Score of 93% or higher
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quiz LTs: 4's on 18 of the 22 LTs, none below a 3
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Final Exam: 4's on 12 of 16 problems
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In order to guarantee a grade of "B", you should attain all of the following:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Preparation and Participation:  Score of 88% or higher
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quiz LTs: 3's or higher on 18 of the 22 LTs, none below a 2
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Final Exam: 3's or higher on 12 of 16 problems
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In order to guarantee a grade of "C", you should attain all of the following:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Preparation and Participation:  Score of 83% or higher
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quiz LTs: 3's or higher on 14 of the 22 LTs, no 0's
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Final Exam: 3's on 8 of 16 problems, 1.5 average or higher on remaining problems
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In order to guarantee a grade of "D", you should attain all of the following:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Preparation and Participation:  Score of 78\% or higher
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quiz LTs: 2's or higher on 14 of the 22 LTs
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Final Exam: 2's on 8 of 16 problems, 1 average or higher on remaining problems
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        If you satisfy all requirements for one of the letter grades and also meet one of the requirements for the next higher letter grade, 
        you will earn a plus (+) grade; for example, a student who meets all three requirements for a "B" and also has a 
        Preparation and Participation score of 96% (which meets the requirements for an "A") will earn a "B+". 
      </p>

      <p>
        If you satisfy two of the three requirements for one of the letter grades as well as the requirement for the third category in the 
        next lower letter grade, you will earn a minus (-) grade; for example, a student who meets the first two requirements for an "A" 
        and also earns 3's or higher on 12 of 16 final exam problems (the third requirement for a "B") will earn a final grade of "A-". 
      </p>

      <p>
        Students who do not earn a "D-" or higher according to the above rules will earn an "E".
      </p>

      <p>
        The instructor reserves the right to change the above requirements for grade determination at any time and for any reason, 
        but only in ways which will favor students!
      </p>
      
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Advantages of Standards Based Grading</title>

      <p>
        This grading system may be unfamiliar to you, and therefore cause some level of confusion or stress. 
        Please try to keep an open mind about this grading system and remember things are not bad simply because they are unfamiliar! 
        From my point of view as a mathematics professor who has taught a vast number of courses for over a decade, 
        including this one multiple times, there are three major advantages of this standards based grading system over a 
        traditional weighted-average system:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            You will have several opportunities to display your understanding of the material. 
            You will be assessed on each of the LTs twice on quizzes and have the ability to reassess on them, 
            so you do not need to worry if you do not completely understand a LT the first time you encounter it! 
            If it takes you a bit longer to grasp a concept, you will not be penalized as long as you make the effort to reassess!
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            This approach stresses working out problems in a completely correct way to emphasize your learning, since doing many tasks poorly is not advantageous. 
            This will help you succeed in your next mathematics class!
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            This system allows you to see the expectations of our course and how well you are meeting those expectations. 
            If you would like to do better in this course, this system will make it clear exactly how you can do better 
            (e.g., study a particular LT on which you have a low score and reassess on it!).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If at any time you are unsure about how you are progressing in this course, I would be more than happy to speak with you about your progress!
      </p>
      
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-132-Course_Policies">
    <title>Course Policies</title>
    
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Extra Credit</title>

      <p>
        There will be no extra credit available in this course. If you are interested in raising your grade, take the time you would have spent on 
        an extra credit assignment and spend it making sure you get more "regular credit" instead; for example, by studying for and scheduling a reassessment!
      </p>
      
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Attendance and Class Recording</title>
      
      <p>
        You are expected to attend each class meeting. 
        If you must miss class, you are responsible for all announcements and material covered. 
        Many of the topics we will cover build upon previously covered topics, so getting behind in this course can be problematic. 
        With that said:  <alert> If you are sick, please do not comes to class!</alert>. 
        I will make sure you do not miss anything! To this end, an attempt will be made to record all class meetings. 
        These recordings will be accessible only to the instructor and students in this course for the purpose of reviewing class 
        discussions and presentations at a later time. These recordings could include your image, voice, and other forms of 
        participation in this course. By enrolling in this course you are consenting to being recorded in this way.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Class Cancelation</title>

      <p>
        If class is canceled for any reason (including WSU closing), you will likely be expected to review some course material on your own before the next class meeting. 
        All expectations will be communicated through email shortly after the cancellation is announced.
      </p>
      
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Email</title>
      
      <p>
        I try to respond to emails within 24 hours; typically, emails received by 4:00pm will receive a response the same day, 
        and those received after 4:00pm will receive a response the following day. You are encouraged to email me regarding any
        issues you are having in this course at any time; however, emails sent within 24 hours of an assessment may not get 
        a response beforehand. So please plan accordingly!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Studying</title>
      
        <p>
          To succeed in this course, you will need to dedicate a significant amount of time to the course. 
          Mathematics is learned by doing, meaning it is important to work numerous problems to fully understand the concepts we cover. 
          The assigned work is the minimum amount you should be completing; if you feel like you don't fully understand a concept, ask
          me for some additional problems. A general rule (for all college courses) is for each hour of class per week you spend a minimum 
          of two hours outside of class on homework, studying, etc. Since this course meets three hours per week, you should plan 
          to spend at least six hours outside of class per week working on math.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accomodations</title>
      
      <p>
        If you anticipate issues related to the format or requirements of this course, please contact me as early as possible to discuss ways to 
        ensure your full participation. If you believe formal, disability-related accommodations are appropriate, it is very important you register 
        with the Student Accessibility Services office and notify me of your eligibility for such accommodations.
        We can then plan how to best coordinate your accommodations.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Academic Integrity and AI Usage</title>

      <p>
        The WSU Mathematics Department does not tolerate academic dishonesty! For an explanation of what is considered “academic dishonesty” at 
        WSU, please see the policy included in the <url href="https://catalog.worcester.edu/undergraduate/academic-policies-procedures/academic-honesty">2025-2026 
        Undergraduate Catalog</url>. All instructors in the WSU Mathematics Department are required to 
        report all incidents of academic dishonesty observed, detected, or otherwise determined to have occurred to the WSU Academic Central File. 
        WSU requires some penalty be imposed for every reported violation of the academic honesty policy. All incidents of academic dishonesty in 
        this course will result in (at least) a score of 0 on that assignment; additional penalties will be imposed at the instructor's discretion 
        and/or as required by the 
        <url href="https://webcdn.worcester.edu/mathematics/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2023/03/Math-Department-Academic-Honesty-Policy.pdf">Mathematics 
        Department Academic Honesty Policy</url>. In this course, instances of academic dishonesty will include (but not limited to!):
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Submitting any assignment (including reading guides) you did not complete yourself, either work produced by a classmate/friend or from an online 
            third party (such as Chegg, Course Hero, etc.). Working with your peers is completely fine and strongly encouraged; 
            however, any submissions should be a result of your own understanding of the subject matter based on this collaboration.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Using course materials, the internet, or a calculator in any unauthorized way during a weekly quiz or the final exam (including looking up answers online, 
            viewing notes/texts/examples on MOM or another site, communicating with other students, etc.).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            AI cannot be used on graded assignments in this course unless otherwise specified.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Instructor Rights</title>
      <p>
        <alert> As the instructor of this course, I reserve the right to make changes to the above course policies at any time. All changes will be announced in class.</alert>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-132-LASC">
    <title>LASC</title>
    <p>
      This is a Quantitative Reasoning (QR) course in the Liberal Arts and Sciences Curriculum (LASC).
      This course will address the following LASC Objectives:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Acquaint students with formal systems, procedures, and sequences of operations.
      </li>
      <li>
        Strengthen students' understanding of variables and functions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Apply mathematical techniques to the analysis and solution of real-life problems.
      </li>
      <li>
        Emphasize the importance of accuracy, including precise language and careful definitions of mathematical concepts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Understand both the underlying principles and practical applications of one or more fields of mathematics.
      </li>
      <li>
        Develop an understanding of and facility with statistical analysis, including understanding of its applications and limitations.
      </li>
      <li>
        Communicate effectively orally and in writing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Understand and employ quantitative and qualitative reasoning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Apply skills in critical thinking.
      </li>
      <li>
        Understand the roles of science and technology in our modern world.
      </li>
      <li>
        Understand how scholars in various disciplines approach problems and construct knowledge.
      </li>
      <li>
        Become socially responsible agents in the world.
      </li>
      <li>
        Make connections across courses and disciplines.
      </li>
      <li>
        Develop as healthy individuals--physically, emotionally, socially, ethically, and intellectually.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-132-StudentLearningOutcomes">
    <title>Student Learning Outcomes</title>
    <p>
      Students will be able to
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Justify their mathematical reasoning verbally, symbolically and in writing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve problems related to standard and nonstandard units of measure and perform unit conversions for length, weight, area, and volume.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use properties of polygons, angles, and lines to solve problems.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        Analyze and write deductive proofs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Solve problems involving area, perimeter, volume, and surface area.
      </li>
      <li>
        Solve problems involving area, perimeter, volume, and surface area.
      </li>

    </ol>
  </subsection>

